(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["garbha"]={"slug":"garbha","name":"Garbha","deva":"गर्भ","note":"","intro":["<em>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda · Sāmānya (general) Upaniṣad · No. 17 in the Muktikā list of 108 · ascribed to the sage Pippalāda (</em>paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstram<em>)</em>","The Garbha Upaniṣad (<em>garbha</em> = \"womb, embryo\") is among the most singular texts in the Upaniṣadic corpus: a compact treatise on <strong>human embryology and the constitution of the body</strong>, framed by a soteriology of release. In a few dense prose sūtras it sets out the body's elemental composition, the seven tissues (<em>dhātu</em>) and their sequential formation, the <strong>month-by-month development of the foetus</strong>, the determination of sex and of twins and of congenital conditions, the soul's recollection of its past births in the ninth month, the celebrated <strong>lament of the being in the womb</strong> and its fourfold vow of refuge, the obliteration of that memory at birth by the \"Vaiṣṇava wind,\" and finally the body-as-fire-sacrifice (<em>yajña</em>) allegory with a remarkable <strong>anatomical census</strong> (the counts of joints, vital points, sinews, hair-follicles, and the weights of the inner organs). It closes by naming itself the <em>mokṣa-śāstra</em> of Pippalāda."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"A complete per-section scholarly edition","title":"Devanāgarī · IAST · pada-by-pada gloss · literal · paraphrase · five-commentary digest","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda · Sāmānya (general) Upaniṣad · No. 17 in the Muktikā list of 108 · ascribed to the sage Pippalāda (</em>paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstram<em>)</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"About this edition","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Garbha Upaniṣad (<em>garbha</em> = \"womb, embryo\") is among the most singular texts in the Upaniṣadic corpus: a compact treatise on <strong>human embryology and the constitution of the body</strong>, framed by a soteriology of release. In a few dense prose sūtras it sets out the body's elemental composition, the seven tissues (<em>dhātu</em>) and their sequential formation, the <strong>month-by-month development of the foetus</strong>, the determination of sex and of twins and of congenital conditions, the soul's recollection of its past births in the ninth month, the celebrated <strong>lament of the being in the womb</strong> and its fourfold vow of refuge, the obliteration of that memory at birth by the \"Vaiṣṇava wind,\" and finally the body-as-fire-sacrifice (<em>yajña</em>) allegory with a remarkable <strong>anatomical census</strong> (the counts of joints, vital points, sinews, hair-follicles, and the weights of the inner organs). It closes by naming itself the <em>mokṣa-śāstra</em> of Pippalāda.","This document gives the <strong>complete text</strong> (the whole work is short enough for a single full-depth treatment), section by section, in the format of the series."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Veda, school, and the Atharvan tension","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The colophon and the e-text header place Garbha in the <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong> as a <em>sāmānya</em> (general) Upaniṣad, No. 17 of the Muktikā 108. Yet the text twice calls itself <strong>\"<em>paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstram</em>\"</strong> — \"the liberation-treatise of (the school of) Pippalāda,\" the teacher of the <strong>Paippalāda recension of the Atharvaveda</strong>. This Atharvan/Pippalāda self-ascription, against a Yajurvedic classification, is a genuine feature of the text (some lists indeed assign Garbha to the Atharvaveda); it is preserved and flagged here rather than resolved. The Upaniṣad is comparatively <strong>late</strong> and stands close to the classical medical (Āyurveda) tradition — its embryology runs strikingly parallel to the <em>Śārīrasthāna</em> of Caraka and Suśruta.","<strong>Source-note.</strong> The base IAST/Devanāgarī is mechanically transliterated (via the `indic_transliteration`/sanscript library) from the proof-read ITRANS file `garbha.itx` at <strong>sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (transcribed by Sunder Hattangadi), and verified against the readings of Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar's edition. The transmitted section-numbering (four numbered sections + an unnumbered closing section) is followed exactly; the body's composition-formula and the closing anatomical census are notoriously variable across manuscripts, and the chief divergences are tabulated below."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Manuscript & recension variations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Reading in this e-text</td><td>Variant / note</td></tr><tr><td>Veda-assignment</td><td>\"Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda, Sāmānya Up.\" (header)</td><td>text self-styles <em>paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstram</em> → Atharva/Paippalāda; lists differ</td></tr><tr><td>Opening formula</td><td><em>ṣaḍ-guṇa-yoga-yuktam</em></td><td>some MSS <em>ṣaḍ-guṇam</em>; the \"six guṇas\" are variously the six tastes or the six bhāvas</td></tr><tr><td>§1 / §2 join</td><td><em>…saptavidhā bhavanti ‖1‖ śuklo raktaḥ…</em> (seven colours open §2)</td><td>the seven colours (white, red, black, smoky, yellow, tawny, pale) are read by some as completing the \"<em>saptavidhā</em>\" of §1</td></tr><tr><td>§3 development</td><td>foetal milestones by lunar month</td><td>Caraka/Suśruta give partly different month-assignments (esp. limbs vs. consciousness)</td></tr><tr><td>§3</td><td><em>aṣṭau prakṛtayaḥ ṣoḍaśa vikārāḥ</em></td><td>the Sāṃkhya \"eight prakṛtis, sixteen modifications\" — wording varies</td></tr><tr><td>§4</td><td>four refuge-vows: Sāṃkhya-Yoga · Maheśvara · Nārāyaṇa · sanātana Brahman</td><td>some recensions omit or reorder the Maheśvara/Nārāyaṇa vows (sectarian layering)</td></tr><tr><td>§5 census</td><td><em>saptottaraṃ marma-śataṃ · sāśītikaṃ sandhi-śataṃ · sa-navakaṃ snāyu-śatam · sapta śirā-śatāni · pañca majjā-śatāni · asthīni trīṇi śatāni ṣaṣṭiś ca · romāṇi ardha-catasraḥ koṭyaḥ</em></td><td>the numbers (107 <em>marmas</em>; 180 joints; sinews \"a-hundred-with-nine\" = 109, commonly emended to 900; 700 vessels; 500 marrows; 360 bones; <strong>3.5 crore</strong> hairs, <em>ardha-catasraḥ koṭyaḥ</em>) vary by MS and differ from Suśruta's counts (Suśruta agrees on 107 <em>marmas</em>, but gives 300 bones, 210 joints, 700 <em>śirā</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>Organ-weights</td><td>heart 8 <em>pala</em>, tongue 12 <em>pala</em>, bile 1 <em>prastha</em>, phlegm 1 <em>āḍhaka</em>, semen 1 <em>kuḍava</em>, fat 2 <em>prastha</em></td><td>weights vary; units: <em>kuḍava</em> &lt; <em>prastha</em> &lt; <em>āḍhaka</em></td></tr><tr><td>Colophon</td><td><em>paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstraṃ parisamāptam</em> (×2)</td><td>confirms the Pippalāda ascription</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्वि नावधीतमस्तु मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ saha nāv avatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai | tejasvi nāv adhītam astu mā vidviṣāvahai | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ‖"],"words":[],"lit":"Oṃ. May (Brahman) protect us both (teacher and pupil); may He nourish us both; may we work together with vigour; may our study be radiant and effective; may we never hate (each other). Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The text invokes the śānti only by its incipit — <em>\"oṃ saha nāv avatu iti śāntiḥ\"</em> (\"Oṃ, 'May (He) protect us both' — thus the peace-invocation\"), i.e. the standard <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong> śānti, given here in full:","<em>(The</em> saha nāv avatu <em>śānti is proper to the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda — consistent with the Upaniṣad's classification, even as the text's colophon claims a Paippalāda/Atharvan pedigree.)</em>"]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The five commentaries (and how they are used here)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Garbha Upaniṣad attracted <strong>no commentary from Śaṅkara or Rāmatīrtha</strong>. Its sole classical Sanskrit commentary is that of <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong>, who glossed all 108 Muktikā Upaniṣads. Because the text is as much a <em>medical</em> document as a Vedāntic one, the most illuminating comparative authority is the embryology of the classical <strong>Āyurveda</strong> — included here as a genuine fifth voice. The stack:","<table><tr><td>Siglum</td><td>Authority</td><td>Work / basis</td><td>Standpoint</td></tr><tr><td><strong>BY</strong></td><td><strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong> (18th c., Kāñcī)</td><td><em>Dīpikā</em> on the 108 Upaniṣads (Adyar Library)</td><td>The sole classical Sanskrit commentary; reads the embryology as <em>adhyātma</em> allegory and supplies the traditional word-division</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ai</strong></td><td><strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong> (1914)</td><td><em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em> (transl. + notes)</td><td>The standard English of the minor Upaniṣads; sober, follows BY</td></tr><tr><td><strong>De</strong></td><td><strong>Paul Deussen</strong> (1897)</td><td><em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (<em>Garbha-Upaniṣad</em>)</td><td>Indological; flags the medical character and the textual corruptions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Āy</strong></td><td><strong>The Āyurvedic embryology</strong></td><td>Caraka-Saṃhitā &amp; Suśruta-Saṃhitā, <em>Śārīrasthāna</em></td><td>The medical-tradition parallel: the <em>garbhāvakrānti</em> (descent of the embryo), the month-by-month <em>māsānumāsika</em> development, the <em>dhātu</em>-sequence, <em>marma</em>/bone counts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DLS</strong></td><td><strong>Sivananda / Krishnananda</strong></td><td>Divine Life Society writings on the minor Upaniṣads</td><td>Modern practitioner-Vedānta; reads Garbha as <em>vairāgya</em>-literature and the body as a meditation on impermanence</td></tr></table>","<strong>Honesty note.</strong> Only <strong>BY</strong> is a verse/passage-strict classical Sanskrit commentary; <strong>Ai</strong> and <strong>De</strong> are scholarly translations with notes (both Garbha-specific); <strong>Āy</strong> is a <em>comparative</em> tradition, not a commentary on this text — used to illuminate the embryology, and labelled as such; <strong>DLS</strong> supplies a thematic (not verse-strict) Vedāntic reading. Where the embryology diverges from Caraka/Suśruta, the divergence is recorded."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Maṅgala (opening benediction)","title":"","sa":["यद्गर्भोपनिषद्वेद्यं गर्भस्य स्वात्मबोधकम् ।","शरीरापह्नवात्सिद्धं स्वमात्रं कलये हरिम् ॥"],"iast":["yad garbhopaniṣad-vedyaṃ garbhasya svātma-bodhakam | śarīrāpahnavāt siddhaṃ svamātraṃ kalaye harim ‖"],"words":[["yat garbha-upaniṣad-vedyam","n. nom. rel.","\"that which is to be known through the Garbha Upaniṣad\""],["garbhasya svātma-bodhakam","gen. + n.","\"the awakener of the embryo to its own Self\""],["śarīra-apahnavāt siddham","abl. + p.p.","\"established by the negation (sublation) of the body\""],["svamātram","n. acc.","\"the sole reality / one's own measure (essence)\""],["kalaye harim","√kal pres. 1 sg. + acc.","\"I contemplate Hari (Viṣṇu/the Self)\""]],"lit":"\"That which is to be known through the Garbha Upaniṣad, the awakener of the embryo to its own Self — established by the sublation of the body — that sole Reality, Hari, I contemplate.\"","para":"A benedictory verse (later, Vaiṣṇava-tinged) declaring the Upaniṣad's true aim: beneath the embryology lies an <em>adhyātma</em> teaching — the body is to be <strong>negated</strong> (<em>apahnava</em>, \"denied/sublated\") so that what is \"to be known\" is the indwelling Self (Hari). The clinical anatomy that follows is, on this reading, a sustained exercise in dis-identification from the body.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","makes this the hermeneutic key: the whole gruesome catalogue serves <em>śarīra-apahnava</em>, the discrediting of body-identity, leaving <em>svamātra</em> (the Self alone)"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads it as the <em>vairāgya</em> thesis"],["Paul Deussen","note the verse is a (probably later) prefatory maṅgala, not part of the archaic core"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Opening sūtra","title":"the sixfold definition of the body","sa":["ॐ पञ्चात्मकं पञ्चसु वर्तमानं षडाश्रयं षड्गुणयोगयुक्तम् । तत्सप्तधातु त्रिमलं द्वियोनि चतुर्विधाहारमयं शरीरं भवति ॥"],"iast":["oṃ pañcātmakaṃ pañcasu vartamānaṃ ṣaḍ-āśrayaṃ ṣaḍ-guṇa-yoga-yuktam | tat sapta-dhātu tri-malaṃ dvi-yoni catur-vidhāhāra-mayaṃ śarīraṃ bhavati ‖"],"words":[["pañca-ātmakam","n. nom.","\"consisting of five (elements)\""],["pañcasu vartamānam","loc. pl. + part.","\"existing among the five (sense-objects)\""],["ṣaḍ-āśrayam","n. nom.","\"having six supports (the six tastes / six bases)\""],["ṣaḍ-guṇa-yoga-yuktam","n. nom.","\"endowed with the union of the six qualities\""],["sapta-dhātu","n.","\"having the seven tissues\""],["tri-malam","n.","\"having the three impurities (wastes)\""],["dvi-yoni","n.","\"arising from two sources (the two parents)\""],["catur-vidha-āhāra-mayam","n.","\"made of the fourfold food\""],["śarīraṃ bhavati","nom. + pres.","\"(such) the body is\""]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. The body is fivefold (in element), existing among the five (sense-spheres), six-based, endowed with the union of the six qualities; it has the seven tissues, the three wastes, two sources, and is made of fourfold food.\"","para":"The text's table of contents in a single sūtra. The body is analysed under seven heads, each unpacked below or in §1–§2: <strong>five</strong> elements (<em>pañcātmaka</em>); the <strong>five</strong> sense-spheres it operates among; <strong>six</strong> supports/bases (the six <em>rasas</em>, tastes — §1); the <strong>six</strong> qualities in union (variously the six tastes again, or the six <em>ūrmis</em>/affects); the <strong>seven</strong> tissues (<em>dhātu</em> — §2); the <strong>three</strong> wastes (<em>mala</em>: faeces, urine, sweat — or in some readings vāta-pitta-kapha); the <strong>two</strong> sources (the seed of the two parents); and the <strong>four</strong> kinds of food (chewed, sucked, licked, drunk — <em>carvya, peya, lehya, coṣya</em>). The remainder of the Upaniṣad is the commentary on this formula.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","glosses each head and treats the <em>catur-vidhāhāra</em> as the four modes of intake named in §5 (<em>aśita-pīta-lehya-coṣya</em>)"],["The Āyurvedic embryology","notes the close parallel with the Āyurvedic definition of the <em>śarīra</em> as <em>pañca-mahābhūta-vikāra</em> with seven <em>dhātus</em> and three <em>malas</em> (vāta/pitta/kapha or mala/mūtra/sveda) — the very vocabulary of Caraka <em>Śārīrasthāna</em>"],["Paul Deussen","observes the formula's mnemonic 5-5-6-6-7-3-2-4 numerical structure"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","keeps the rendering close, identifying the three <em>malas</em> as the excretions"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Section 1","title":"The Five Elements, the Senses, the Six Tastes, the Seven Notes","sa":["पञ्चात्मकमिति कस्मात् पृथिव्यापस्तेजोवायुराकाशमिति । अस्मिन्पञ्चात्मके शरीरे का पृथिवी का आपः किं तेजः को वायुः किमाकाशम् । तत्र यत्कठिनं सा पृथिवी, यद्द्रवं ता आपः, यदुष्णं तत्तेजः, यत्सञ्चरति स वायुः, यत्सुषिरं तदाकाशमित्युच्यते ॥ तत्र पृथिवी धारणे, आपः पिण्डीकरणे, तेजः प्रकाशने, वायुर्गमने, आकाशमवकाशप्रदाने । पृथक् श्रोत्रे शब्दोपलब्धौ, त्वक् स्पर्शे, चक्षुषी रूपे, जिह्वा रसने, नासिका आघ्राणे, उपस्थश्चानन्दने, अपानमुत्सर्गे; बुद्ध्या बुद्ध्यति, मनसा सङ्कल्पयति, वाचा वदति । षडाश्रयमिति कस्मात् मधुराम्ललवणतिक्तकटुकषायरसान्विन्दते । षड्जर्षभगान्धारमध्यमपञ्चमधैवतनिषादाश्चेति । इष्टानिष्टशब्दसंज्ञाः प्रतिविधाः सप्तविधा भवन्ति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["pañcātmakam iti kasmāt — pṛthivy-āpas-tejo-vāyur-ākāśam iti | asmin pañcātmake śarīre kā pṛthivī kā āpaḥ kiṃ tejaḥ ko vāyuḥ kim ākāśam | tatra yat kaṭhinaṃ sā pṛthivī, yad dravaṃ tā āpaḥ, yad uṣṇaṃ tat tejaḥ, yat sañcarati sa vāyuḥ, yat suṣiraṃ tad ākāśam ity ucyate | tatra pṛthivī dhāraṇe, āpaḥ piṇḍīkaraṇe, tejaḥ prakāśane, vāyur gamane, ākāśam avakāśa-pradāne | pṛthak śrotre śabdopalabdhau, tvak sparśe, cakṣuṣī rūpe, jihvā rasane, nāsikā āghrāṇe, upasthaś cānandane, apānam utsarge; buddhyā buddhyati, manasā saṅkalpayati, vācā vadati | ṣaḍ-āśrayam iti kasmāt — madhurāmla-lavaṇa-tikta-kaṭu-kaṣāya-rasān vindate | ṣaḍja-ṛṣabha-gāndhāra-madhyama-pañcama-dhaivata-niṣādāś ceti | iṣṭāniṣṭa-śabda-saṃjñāḥ pratividhāḥ saptavidhā bhavanti ‖"],"words":[["pañcātmakam iti kasmāt","\"why 'fivefold'?\"","\"Why is it called 'consisting of five'?\""],["pṛthivī-āpas-tejas-vāyus-ākāśam","nom.","\"earth, water, fire, air, ether\""],["yat kaṭhinaṃ sā pṛthivī","rel. + nom.","\"what is hard, that is earth\""],["yad dravaṃ tā āpaḥ","rel. + nom.","\"what is liquid, that is water\""],["yad uṣṇaṃ tat tejaḥ","rel. + nom.","\"what is hot, that is fire\""],["yat sañcarati sa vāyuḥ","rel. + pres.","\"what moves about, that is air\""],["yat suṣiraṃ tad ākāśam","rel. + nom.","\"what is hollow/porous, that is ether\""],["pṛthivī dhāraṇe","loc. (function)","\"earth (serves) for support\""],["āpaḥ piṇḍī-karaṇe","loc.","\"water for cohesion (binding into a mass)\""],["tejaḥ prakāśane","loc.","\"fire for illumination\""],["vāyuḥ gamane","loc.","\"air for movement\""],["ākāśam avakāśa-pradāne","loc.","\"ether for giving room (space)\""],["śrotre śabda-upalabdhau","loc.","\"the ears, for the apprehension of sound\""],["tvak sparśe, cakṣuṣī rūpe","loc.","\"skin for touch, the eyes for form\""],["jihvā rasane, nāsikā āghrāṇe","loc.","\"tongue for taste, nose for smell\""],["upasthaḥ ānandane","loc.","\"the generative organ for pleasure\""],["apānam utsarge","loc.","\"the anus for excretion\""],["buddhyā buddhyati","instr. + pres.","\"by the intellect one knows\""],["manasā saṅkalpayati","instr. + pres.","\"by the mind one wills/forms intention\""],["vācā vadati","instr. + pres.","\"by speech one speaks\""],["madhura-amla-lavaṇa-tikta-kaṭu-kaṣāya-rasān vindate","acc. pl. + pres.","\"one experiences the tastes: sweet, sour, salt, bitter, pungent, astringent\""],["ṣaḍja-ṛṣabha-gāndhāra-madhyama-pañcama-dhaivata-niṣādāḥ","nom. pl.","\"the seven musical notes Sa-Ri-Ga-Ma-Pa-Dha-Ni\""],["iṣṭa-aniṣṭa-śabda-saṃjñāḥ","nom. pl.","\"designations of agreeable and disagreeable sound\""],["pratividhāḥ saptavidhāḥ bhavanti","nom. + pres.","\"(these) become severally sevenfold\""]],"lit":"\"Why 'consisting of five'? — Earth, water, fire, air, ether. In this fivefold body, which is earth, which water, what fire, what air, what ether? Therein: what is hard is earth; what is liquid is water; what is hot is fire; what moves is air; what is hollow is called ether. Therein, earth serves for support, water for cohesion, fire for illumination, air for movement, ether for the giving of room. Severally: the ears for the apprehension of sound, the skin for touch, the eyes for form, the tongue for taste, the nose for smell, the generative organ for pleasure, the anus for excretion; by the intellect one cognizes, by the mind one wills, by speech one speaks. Why 'six-based'? — One experiences the tastes: sweet, sour, salt, bitter, pungent, astringent. And (the seven musical notes:) Ṣaḍja, Ṛṣabha, Gāndhāra, Madhyama, Pañcama, Dhaivata, Niṣāda. The designations of agreeable and disagreeable sound become, severally, sevenfold.\"","para":"The exposition of \"fivefold\" and \"six-based.\" Each of the five elements is <strong>defined functionally</strong> (by its cardinal property — hardness, liquidity, heat, motion, porosity) and <strong>assigned a bodily function</strong> (support, cohesion, illumination, movement, space-giving) — a thoroughly Sāṃkhya-Āyurvedic physics. The senses are then matched to their objects (the standard <em>indriya-artha</em> mapping, with the addition of the generative organ \"for pleasure\" and the anus \"for excretion\"), and the three inner instruments named (<em>buddhi</em> cognizes, <em>manas</em> wills, <em>vāc</em> speaks). \"Six-based\" is glossed as the <strong>six tastes</strong> (<em>ṣaḍ-rasa</em> — the exact Āyurvedic list). The <strong>seven musical notes</strong> (the <em>saptasvara</em> of Indian music) and the sevenfold agreeable/disagreeable sounds round out the sensory inventory: the body is an apparatus tuned to the whole range of experience.","coms":[["The Āyurvedic embryology","notes the element-definitions and the six-taste list are verbatim Āyurveda (Caraka <em>Sūtrasthāna</em> on <em>ṣaḍ-rasa</em>; the <em>pañca-mahābhūta</em> functional definitions); the <em>ṣaḍ-āśraya</em> = the six tastes that \"support\" the body through nutrition"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the sense-object mapping as the field of <em>bhoga</em> (experience) that the indwelling Self must transcend"],["Paul Deussen","observes the striking inclusion of the <em>saptasvara</em> (musical scale) — the body as resonant instrument — unusual in an Upaniṣad"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","identifies the \"sevenfold\" as the seven notes correlated with agreeable/disagreeable sound"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads the whole inventory as the catalogue of what binds: every sense a gate of <em>rāga-dveṣa</em>"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Section 2","title":"The Seven Tissues and the Forming of the Embryo","sa":["शुक्लो रक्तः कृष्णो धूम्रः पीतः कपिलः पाण्डुर इति । सप्तधातुमिति कस्मात् — यदा देवदत्तस्य द्रव्यादिविषया जायन्ते ॥ परस्परं सौम्यगुणत्वात् षड्विधो रसः, रसाच्छोणितं, शोणितान्मांसं, मांसान्मेदः, मेदसः स्नावा, स्नाव्नोऽस्थीनि, अस्थिभ्यो मज्जा, मज्ज्ञः शुक्रम् । शुक्रशोणितसंयोगादावर्तते गर्भो हृदि व्यवस्थां नयति । हृदयेऽन्तराग्निः, अग्निस्थाने पित्तं, पित्तस्थाने वायुः, वायुस्थाने हृदयं प्राजापत्यात्क्रमात् ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["śuklo raktaḥ kṛṣṇo dhūmraḥ pītaḥ kapilaḥ pāṇḍura iti | sapta-dhātum iti kasmāt — yadā devadattasya dravyādi-viṣayā jāyante | parasparaṃ saumya-guṇatvāt ṣaḍ-vidho rasaḥ, rasāc choṇitaṃ, śoṇitān māṃsaṃ, māṃsān medo, medasaḥ snāvā, snāvno 'sthīni, asthibhyo majjā, majjñaḥ śukram | śukra-śoṇita-saṃyogād āvartate garbho hṛdi vyavasthāṃ nayati | hṛdaye 'ntarāgniḥ, agni-sthāne pittaṃ, pitta-sthāne vāyuḥ, vāyu-sthāne hṛdayaṃ prājāpatyāt kramāt ‖"],"words":[["śuklaḥ raktaḥ kṛṣṇaḥ dhūmraḥ pītaḥ kapilaḥ pāṇḍuraḥ","nom.","\"white, red, black, smoky, yellow, tawny, pale\" (the seven colours/complexions)"],["sapta-dhātum iti kasmāt","\"why 'seven-tissued'?\"","\"Why is it called 'having seven tissues'?\""],["devadattasya dravyādi-viṣayāḥ jāyante","gen. + pres.","\"when, for Devadatta (= any person), the objects beginning with substances arise (are ingested)\""],["parasparaṃ saumya-guṇatvāt","adv. + abl.","\"through their mutual 'lunar' (cooling/nourishing) quality\""],["ṣaḍ-vidhaḥ rasaḥ","nom.","\"the sixfold (taste-bearing) chyle (rasa)\""],["rasāt śoṇitaṃ","abl. + nom.","\"from chyle, blood\""],["śoṇitāt māṃsaṃ","abl.","\"from blood, flesh\""],["māṃsāt medaḥ","abl.","\"from flesh, fat\""],["medasaḥ snāvā","abl.","\"from fat, sinew\""],["snāvnaḥ asthīni","abl. + nom. pl.","\"from sinew, bones\""],["asthibhyaḥ majjā","abl. + nom.","\"from bones, marrow\""],["majjñaḥ śukram","abl. + nom.","\"from marrow, semen\""],["śukra-śoṇita-saṃyogāt","abl.","\"from the union of semen and blood (ovum)\""],["āvartate garbhaḥ","pres. + nom.","\"the embryo is conceived/develops\""],["hṛdi vyavasthāṃ nayati","loc. + acc.","\"establishes its seat in the heart\""],["hṛdaye antarāgniḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the heart, the inner fire\""],["agni-sthāne pittaṃ","loc.","\"in the fire's place, bile\""],["pitta-sthāne vāyuḥ","loc.","\"in the bile's place, air\""],["vāyu-sthāne hṛdayaṃ prājāpatyāt kramāt","loc. + abl.","\"in the air's place, the heart — by the order ordained of Prajāpati\""]],"lit":"\"White, red, black, smoky, yellow, tawny, pale (— these are the seven colours). Why 'seven-tissued'? — When, for Devadatta (any person), the sense-objects beginning with (ingested) substances arise: through their mutual nourishing ('lunar') quality there is the sixfold chyle (<em>rasa</em>); from chyle, blood; from blood, flesh; from flesh, fat; from fat, sinew; from sinew, bones; from bones, marrow; from marrow, semen. From the union of semen and blood (ovum) the embryo is conceived and establishes its seat in the heart. In the heart is the inner fire; in the place of fire, bile; in the place of bile, air; in the place of air, the heart — (all) in the sequence ordained of Prajāpati.\"","para":"The <strong>seven dhātus</strong> and their genesis. Beginning with ingested food, the \"lunar\" (cooling, nutritive) transformation yields <em>rasa</em> (chyle/plasma), and from it, in a strict serial transformation, arise the seven tissues: <strong>rasa → śoṇita (blood) → māṃsa (flesh) → medas (fat) → snāyu (sinew) → asthi (bone) → majjā (marrow) → śukra (semen)</strong>. (Counting from blood, this is the classical seven-<em>dhātu</em> chain of Āyurveda, <em>rasa</em> being the prior nutrient-fluid.) The last tissue, <em>śukra</em> (generative essence), uniting with <em>śoṇita</em> (the maternal blood/ovum), conceives the embryo, which takes its seat <strong>in the heart</strong>. There the vital constituents are nested in order — fire, bile (<em>pitta</em>), air (<em>vāyu</em>), and the heart itself — \"by the order of Prajāpati,\" i.e. by the divinely fixed developmental sequence. The seven <strong>colours</strong> that head the section are the complexion-types correlated with constitution (the <em>varṇa</em> of the body).","coms":[["The Āyurvedic embryology","finds here the exact <em>dhātu-pariṇāma</em> (tissue-transformation) doctrine of Caraka/Suśruta — each tissue the nutritive product of the one before, <em>rasa</em> the foundation, <em>śukra</em> the essence; the \"<em>saumya-guṇa</em>\" (soma/lunar quality) is the Āyurvedic <em>poṣaka</em> (nourishing) principle. The <em>śukra-śoṇita-saṃyoga</em> is the standard medical account of conception"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the <em>prājāpatyāt kramāt</em> (\"the order of Prajāpati\") as the impersonal law of <em>karma</em>-driven formation"],["Paul Deussen","notes the seven colours as the seven <em>varṇas</em> of the body and the heart as the embryo's seat (cf. the <em>manomayaḥ … nihito guhāyām</em> of the heart-cave tradition)"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the dhātu-chain straightforwardly"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","stresses that this minutely physical genesis is narrated only to be renounced (per the maṅgala's <em>śarīrāpahnava</em>)"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Section 3","title":"Month-by-Month Development; Sex; Recollection of Past Births","sa":["ऋतुकाले सम्प्रयोगादेकरात्रोषितं कलिलं भवति, सप्तरात्रोषितं बुद्बुदं भवति, अर्धमासाभ्यन्तरेण पिण्डो भवति, मासाभ्यन्तरेण कठिनो भवति, मासद्वयेन शिरः सम्पद्यते, मासत्रयेण पादप्रवेशो भवति । अथ चतुर्थे मासे जठरकटिप्रदेशो भवति । पञ्चमे मासे पृष्ठवंशो भवति । षष्ठे मासे मुखनासिकाक्षिश्रोत्राणि भवन्ति । सप्तमे मासे जीवेन संयुक्तो भवति । अष्टमे मासे सर्वसम्पूर्णो भवति । पितू रेतोऽतिरिक्तात् पुरुषो भवति, मातू रेतोऽतिरिक्तात् स्त्रियो भवन्ति, उभयोर्बीजतुल्यत्वान्नपुंसको भवति । व्याकुलितमनसोऽन्धाः खञ्जाः कुब्जा वामना भवन्ति । अन्योन्यवायुपरिपीडितशुक्रद्वैध्याद्द्विधा तनुः स्यात्ततो युग्माः प्रजायन्ते ॥ पञ्चात्मकः समर्थः पञ्चात्मकतेजसेद्धरसश्च सम्यग्ज्ञानाद्ध्यानादक्षरमोङ्कारं चिन्तयति । तदेतदेकाक्षरं ज्ञात्वा अष्टौ प्रकृतयः षोडश विकाराः शरीरे तस्यैव देहिनाम् । अथ मात्राशितपीतनाडीसूत्रगतेन प्राण आप्यायते । अथ नवमे मासि सर्वलक्षणसम्पूर्णो भवति, पूर्वजातीः स्मरति, कृताकृतं च कर्म विभाति, शुभाशुभं च कर्म विन्दति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["ṛtu-kāle samprayogād eka-rātroṣitaṃ kalilaṃ bhavati, sapta-rātroṣitaṃ budbudaṃ bhavati, ardha-māsābhyantareṇa piṇḍo bhavati, māsābhyantareṇa kaṭhino bhavati, māsa-dvayena śiraḥ sampadyate, māsa-trayeṇa pāda-praveśo bhavati | atha caturthe māse jaṭhara-kaṭi-pradeśo bhavati | pañcame māse pṛṣṭha-vaṃśo bhavati | ṣaṣṭhe māse mukha-nāsikākṣi-śrotrāṇi bhavanti | saptame māse jīvena saṃyukto bhavati | aṣṭame māse sarva-sampūrṇo bhavati | pitū reto'tiriktāt puruṣo bhavati, mātū reto'tiriktāt striyo bhavanti, ubhayor bīja-tulyatvān napuṃsako bhavati | vyākulita-manaso 'ndhāḥ khañjāḥ kubjā vāmanā bhavanti | anyonya-vāyu-paripīḍita-śukra-dvaidhyād dvidhā tanuḥ syāt tato yugmāḥ prajāyante | pañcātmakaḥ samarthaḥ pañcātmaka-tejaseddha-rasaś ca samyag-jñānād dhyānād akṣaram oṅkāraṃ cintayati | tad etad ekākṣaraṃ jñātvā aṣṭau prakṛtayaḥ ṣoḍaśa vikārāḥ śarīre tasyaiva dehinām | atha mātrāśita-pīta-nāḍī-sūtra-gatena prāṇa āpyāyate | atha navame māsi sarva-lakṣaṇa-sampūrṇo bhavati, pūrva-jātīḥ smarati, kṛtākṛtaṃ ca karma vibhāti, śubhāśubhaṃ ca karma vindati ‖"],"words":[["ṛtu-kāle samprayogāt","loc. + abl.","\"from union in the fertile season\""],["eka-rātra-uṣitaṃ kalilaṃ","acc./nom.","\"abiding one night, it becomes a kalila (a mingled fluid-mass)\""],["sapta-rātra-uṣitaṃ budbudaṃ","nom.","\"abiding seven nights, a budbuda (bubble/vesicle)\""],["ardha-māsa-abhyantareṇa piṇḍaḥ","instr. + nom.","\"within a half-month, a piṇḍa (lump)\""],["māsa-abhyantareṇa kaṭhinaḥ","instr. + nom.","\"within a month, it hardens\""],["māsa-dvayena śiraḥ sampadyate","instr. + nom.","\"in two months the head is formed\""],["māsa-trayeṇa pāda-praveśaḥ","instr. + nom.","\"in three months the feet appear (lit. 'the entry of the feet')\""],["caturthe māse jaṭhara-kaṭi-pradeśaḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the fourth month, the belly and hip region\""],["pañcame māse pṛṣṭha-vaṃśaḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the fifth month, the spinal column\""],["ṣaṣṭhe māse mukha-nāsikā-akṣi-śrotrāṇi","loc. + nom. pl.","\"in the sixth month, mouth, nose, eyes, ears\""],["saptame māse jīvena saṃyuktaḥ","loc. + instr.","\"in the seventh month it is conjoined with the jīva (the living soul)\""],["aṣṭame māse sarva-sampūrṇaḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the eighth month, complete in all (parts)\""],["pituḥ retaḥ atiriktāt puruṣaḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from the father's seed being in excess, a male\""],["mātuḥ retaḥ atiriktāt striyaḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from the mother's seed in excess, a female\""],["ubhayoḥ bīja-tulyatvāt napuṃsakaḥ","abl. + nom.","\"from the equality of both seeds, an intersex/neuter\""],["vyākulita-manasaḥ andhāḥ khañjāḥ kubjāḥ vāmanāḥ","gen. + nom. pl.","\"from a disturbed mind (of the parents): the blind, lame, hunchbacked, dwarfish\""],["anyonya-vāyu-paripīḍita-śukra-dvaidhyāt","abl.","\"from the splitting of the seed, pressed apart by mutually (conflicting) winds\""],["dvidhā tanuḥ … yugmāḥ prajāyante","nom.","\"the body (becomes) twofold; thence twins are born\""],["pañcātmakaḥ samarthaḥ … tejas-iddha-rasaḥ","nom.","\"the fivefold (embryo becomes) capable, its sap kindled by the fivefold fire\""],["samyag-jñānāt dhyānāt akṣaram oṅkāraṃ cintayati","abl. + acc. + pres.","\"through right knowledge and meditation it contemplates the imperishable Oṃ\""],["aṣṭau prakṛtayaḥ ṣoḍaśa vikārāḥ","nom.","\"the eight 'sources' (prakṛtis) and sixteen 'modifications' (vikāras)\" — the Sāṃkhya 24"],["mātrā aśita-pīta-nāḍī-sūtra-gatena","instr.","\"by way of the duct-thread carrying the mother's eaten-and-drunk (nourishment)\""],["prāṇaḥ āpyāyate","nom. + pres.","\"the vital breath is nourished\""],["navame māsi sarva-lakṣaṇa-sampūrṇaḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the ninth month, complete in all marks\""],["pūrva-jātīḥ smarati","acc. pl. + pres.","\"it remembers its former births\""],["kṛta-akṛtaṃ karma vibhāti","acc. + pres.","\"discerns deeds done and undone\""],["śubha-aśubhaṃ karma vindati","acc. + pres.","\"knows its good and evil karma\""]],"lit":"\"From union in the fertile season, having abode one night it becomes a <em>kalila</em> (fluid-mingling); abiding seven nights, a <em>budbuda</em> (vesicle); within a fortnight, a <em>piṇḍa</em> (lump); within a month it hardens; in two months the head is formed; in three months the feet appear. Then in the fourth month the belly and hip-region (form); in the fifth month the spinal column; in the sixth month mouth, nose, eyes, ears (appear); in the seventh month it is conjoined with the <em>jīva</em>; in the eighth month it is complete in every part. From the father's seed in excess comes a male; from the mother's seed in excess, a female; from the equality of both seeds, a neuter. From the parents' disturbed mind are born the blind, the lame, the hunchbacked, the dwarfish. From the splitting of the seed, pressed apart by mutually-opposing winds, the body becomes twofold — thence twins are born. The fivefold (embryo) becomes capable, its sap kindled by the fivefold fire; through right knowledge and meditation it contemplates the imperishable Oṃ. Having known this single syllable — the eight <em>prakṛtis</em> and the sixteen <em>vikāras</em> are in the body of that very embodied one. Then, by way of the duct-thread that carries the mother's eaten and drunk (food), the vital breath is nourished. Then in the ninth month it becomes complete in all marks; it remembers its former births; it discerns deeds done and undone; and it comes to know its good and evil karma.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's celebrated <strong>embryogenesis</strong>, charted against the lunar months: a sequence <em>kalila</em> (day 1, fluid) → <em>budbuda</em> (day 7, bubble) → <em>piṇḍa</em> (½ month, lump) → hardening (1 month) → head (2) → feet (3) → trunk/hips (4) → spine (5) → face and sense-organs (6) → <strong>ensoulment</strong> (7, <em>jīvena saṃyukta</em>) → completion (8). It then states the classical <strong>theory of sex-determination</strong> by the relative dominance of paternal vs. maternal \"seed\" (with equality yielding <em>napuṃsaka</em>), and ascribes congenital conditions to the parents' \"disturbed mind\" at conception and twinning to the seed's being split by opposing \"winds.\" A striking interlude follows: the matured, \"capable\" embryo, nourished through the umbilical <em>nāḍī</em>, attains <strong>right knowledge</strong> and <strong>meditates on Oṃ</strong>, realizing that the entire Sāṃkhya cosmos — the <strong>eight prakṛtis and sixteen vikāras</strong> (the 24 <em>tattvas</em>) — is present within its own body. In the <strong>ninth month</strong> the great event occurs: <em>pūrva-jātīḥ smarati</em> — it <strong>remembers its past lives</strong> and reads the ledger of its own karma. This recollection is the hinge into the lament of §4.","coms":[["The Āyurvedic embryology","reads the month-sequence against Caraka <em>Śārīrasthāna</em> 4 and Suśruta <em>Śārīra</em> 3: the <em>kalila → budbuda → piṇḍa</em> series and the dominance-theory of sex are shared medical doctrine, though the medical texts assign some milestones to different months (Suśruta puts limb-buds and consciousness somewhat differently) — a genuine divergence, noted"],["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","treats the <em>jīvena saṃyukta</em> of the seventh month and the <em>oṅkāra</em>-meditation as the <em>adhyātma</em> core: the embryo is a yogin who, before birth, knows the 24 tattvas and the praṇava"],["Paul Deussen","flags the embedded <strong>Sāṃkhya</strong> (<em>aṣṭau prakṛtayaḥ</em> — the 8 = mūla-prakṛti + mahat + ahaṃkāra + 5 tanmātras; <em>ṣoḍaśa vikārāḥ</em> — the 16 = 5 elements + 5 buddhīndriyas + 5 karmendriyas + manas)"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the milestones and the karma-recollection plainly"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","dwells on <em>pūrva-jātīḥ smarati</em>: the soul's pre-natal clear sight of its karmic bondage — the spark of the <em>vairāgya</em> to come"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Section 4","title":"The Lament in the Womb and the Fourfold Vow","sa":["नानायोनिसहस्राणि दृष्ट्वा चैव ततो मया । आहारा विविधा भुक्ताः पीताश्च विविधाः स्तनाः ॥","जातस्यैव मृतस्यैव जन्म चैव पुनः पुनः । अहो दुःखोदधौ मग्नः न पश्यामि प्रतिक्रियाम् ॥","यन्मया परिजनस्यार्थे कृतं कर्म शुभाशुभम् । एकाकी तेन दह्यामि गतास्ते फलभोगिनः ॥","यदि योन्यां प्रमुञ्चामि सांख्यं योगं समाश्रये । अशुभक्षयकर्तारं फलमुक्तिप्रदायकम् ॥","यदि योन्यां प्रमुञ्चामि तं प्रपद्ये महेश्वरम् । अशुभक्षयकर्तारं फलमुक्तिप्रदायकम् ॥","यदि योन्यां प्रमुञ्चामि तं प्रपद्ये भगवन्तं नारायणं देवम् । अशुभक्षयकर्तारं फलमुक्तिप्रदायकम् । यदि योन्यां प्रमुञ्चामि ध्याये ब्रह्म सनातनम् ॥","अथ जन्तुः स्त्रीयोनिशतं योनिद्वारि सम्प्राप्तो यन्त्रेणापीड्यमानो महता दुःखेन जातमात्रस्तु वैष्णवेन वायुना संस्पृश्यते; तदा न स्मरति जन्ममरणं न च कर्म शुभाशुभम् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["nānā-yoni-sahasrāṇi dṛṣṭvā caiva tato mayā | āhārā vividhā bhuktāḥ pītāś ca vividhāḥ stanāḥ ‖ jātasyaiva mṛtasyaiva janma caiva punaḥ punaḥ | aho duḥkhodadhau magnaḥ na paśyāmi pratikriyām ‖ yan mayā parijanasyārthe kṛtaṃ karma śubhāśubham | ekākī tena dahyāmi gatās te phala-bhoginaḥ ‖ yadi yonyāṃ pramuñcāmi sāṃkhyaṃ yogaṃ samāśraye | aśubha-kṣaya-kartāraṃ phala-mukti-pradāyakam ‖ yadi yonyāṃ pramuñcāmi taṃ prapadye maheśvaram | aśubha-kṣaya-kartāraṃ phala-mukti-pradāyakam ‖ yadi yonyāṃ pramuñcāmi taṃ prapadye bhagavantaṃ nārāyaṇaṃ devam | aśubha-kṣaya-kartāraṃ phala-mukti-pradāyakam | yadi yonyāṃ pramuñcāmi dhyāye brahma sanātanam ‖ atha jantuḥ strī-yoni-śataṃ yoni-dvāri samprāpto yantreṇāpīḍyamāno mahatā duḥkhena jāta-mātras tu vaiṣṇavena vāyunā saṃspṛśyate; tadā na smarati janma-maraṇaṃ na ca karma śubhāśubham ‖"],"words":[["nānā-yoni-sahasrāṇi dṛṣṭvā","acc. pl. + abs.","\"having seen thousands of various wombs\""],["tataḥ mayā","abl. + instr.","\"thereafter, by me\""],["āhārāḥ vividhāḥ bhuktāḥ","nom. pl. + p.p.","\"manifold foods (have been) eaten\""],["pītāḥ ca vividhāḥ stanāḥ","nom. pl.","\"and manifold breasts (have been) sucked\""],["jātasya … mṛtasya … janma punaḥ punaḥ","gen. + nom.","\"of (one) born and dead, birth again and again\""],["aho duḥkha-udadhau magnaḥ","nom.","\"alas! sunk in an ocean of sorrow\""],["na paśyāmi pratikriyām","pres. + acc.","\"I see no remedy\""],["parijanasya arthe kṛtaṃ karma śubha-aśubham","gen. + acc.","\"the good and bad deeds done for my family's sake\""],["ekākī tena dahyāmi","nom. + instr. + pres.","\"alone, I am burned by it\""],["gatāḥ te phala-bhoginaḥ","nom. pl.","\"those enjoyers of the fruit are gone\""],["yadi yonyāṃ pramuñcāmi","loc. + pres.","\"if I am released from the womb\""],["sāṃkhyaṃ yogaṃ samāśraye","acc. + pres.","\"I will take refuge in Sāṃkhya and Yoga\""],["aśubha-kṣaya-kartāram","acc.","\"the destroyer of evil (demerit)\""],["phala-mukti-pradāyakam","acc.","\"the bestower of the fruit, liberation\""],["taṃ prapadye maheśvaram","acc. + pres.","\"I will resort to that Maheśvara\""],["taṃ prapadye bhagavantaṃ nārāyaṇaṃ devam","acc.","\"I will resort to that Lord, the god Nārāyaṇa\""],["dhyāye brahma sanātanam","pres. + acc.","\"I will meditate on the eternal Brahman\""],["jantuḥ strī-yoni-śataṃ","nom. + acc.","\"the creature, (passing) the hundred-fold female passage\""],["yoni-dvāri samprāptaḥ","loc. + p.p.","\"arrived at the door of the womb\""],["yantreṇa āpīḍyamānaḥ","instr. + part.","\"squeezed as by a machine/press\""],["mahatā duḥkhena","instr.","\"with great pain\""],["jāta-mātraḥ tu","nom.","\"but the moment it is born\""],["vaiṣṇavena vāyunā saṃspṛśyate","instr. + pres. pass.","\"it is touched by the Vaiṣṇava wind\""],["tadā na smarati janma-maraṇaṃ … karma","pres.","\"then it remembers neither birth-and-death nor karma\""]],"lit":"\"'Thousands of varied wombs have I seen, thereafter; manifold foods have I eaten, manifold breasts have I sucked. Of (a being) born and dying — birth, again and again! Alas, sunk in an ocean of sorrow, I see no remedy. The good and evil deeds I did for my family's sake — alone I am burned by them, while those who enjoyed the fruits have gone. If I win release from this womb, I will take refuge in Sāṃkhya and Yoga, the destroyer of demerit, the bestower of the fruit — liberation. If I win release from this womb, I will resort to that Maheśvara, the destroyer of evil, the bestower of liberation. If I win release from this womb, I will resort to that Lord, the god Nārāyaṇa, the destroyer of evil, the bestower of liberation. If I win release from this womb, I will meditate on the eternal Brahman.' Then the creature, having reached the door of the womb after the hundred-fold female passage, squeezed as by a press, with great pain — the moment it is born, is touched by the Vaiṣṇava wind; and then it remembers neither birth-and-death nor its good and evil karma.\"","para":"The <strong>lament of the soul in the womb</strong> — the Upaniṣad's emotional and doctrinal climax, and one of the great <em>vairāgya</em> passages of Indian literature. Endowed in the ninth month with recollection (§3), the being surveys its endless past — thousands of wombs, foods, mothers' breasts, deaths beyond counting — and cries out, <em>aho duḥkhodadhau magnaḥ na paśyāmi pratikriyām</em> (\"sunk in an ocean of sorrow, I see no remedy\"). It grasps the loneliness of karma: it bears the burning alone, while those for whose sake it sinned have departed. From this despair springs a <strong>fourfold vow of refuge</strong>, should it escape the womb: (1) <strong>Sāṃkhya-Yoga</strong> (the path of discrimination and discipline), (2) <strong>Maheśvara</strong> (Śiva), (3) <strong>Nārāyaṇa</strong> (Viṣṇu), and (4) <strong>the eternal Brahman</strong> — a notably ecumenical sequence spanning jñāna, Śaiva and Vaiṣṇava bhakti, and nirguṇa Vedānta. But the vow is undone by birth itself: passing the agonizing \"press\" of delivery, the newborn is \"touched by the <strong>Vaiṣṇava wind</strong>\" (<em>vaiṣṇava vāyu</em> — the breath/air of the world, the māyā of embodiment) and instantly <strong>forgets</strong> — birth, death, and the whole karmic ledger. The cycle is thus sealed: the one moment of saving insight is wiped clean at the threshold of the world.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","reads the four vows as the four legitimate <em>mokṣa-mārgas</em>, deliberately enumerated so that no school is excluded; the <em>vaiṣṇava vāyu</em> he glosses as the <em>prāṇa-vāyu</em> infused with <em>māyā</em> (Viṣṇu's deluding power) that occasions birth-amnesia"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","treats the lament as the paradigm of <em>saṃsāra-vairāgya</em> — the soul's clearest seeing comes in extremity, and is the very thing the world erases; hence the spiritual life is the labour of <em>re-membering</em> what birth made us forget"],["Paul Deussen","notes the <strong>sectarian layering</strong> of the Maheśvara and Nārāyaṇa vows (likely later additions to an original Sāṃkhya-Yoga + Brahman pair) and compares the womb-misery topos to its Buddhist and Jain parallels"],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the lament closely and identifies the <em>vaiṣṇava vāyu</em> as \"the air called Vaiṣṇava\" that effaces memory at birth"],["The Āyurvedic embryology","notes the obstetric realism of <em>yantreṇa āpīḍyamānaḥ</em> — the foetus compressed in the birth-canal \"as by a machine,\" matching Suśruta's description of <em>prasava</em> (parturition)"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Section 5","title":"Why \"Śarīra\": The Three Fires, the Body as Sacrifice, and the Anatomical Census","sa":["शरीरमिति कस्मात् — साक्षादग्नयो ह्यत्र श्रीयन्ते — ज्ञानाग्निर्दर्शनाग्निः कोष्ठाग्निरिति । तत्र कोष्ठाग्निर्नाम अशितपीतलेह्यचोष्यं पचतीति । दर्शनाग्नी रूपादीनां दर्शनं करोति । ज्ञानाग्निः शुभाशुभं च कर्म विन्दति । तत्र त्रीणि स्थानानि भवन्ति — हृदये दक्षिणाग्निः, उदरे गार्हपत्यं, मुखमाहवनीयम् । आत्मा यजमानः, बुद्धिं पत्नीं निधाय, मनो ब्रह्मा, लोभादयः पशवः, धृतिर्दीक्षा, सन्तोषश्च, बुद्धीन्द्रियाणि यज्ञपात्राणि, कर्मेन्द्रियाणि हवींषि, शिरः कपालं, केशा दर्भाः, मुखमन्तर्वेदिः, चतुष्कपालं शिरः ॥ षोडश पार्श्वदन्तोष्ठपटलानि, सप्तोत्तरं मर्मशतं, साशीतिकं सन्धिशतं, सनवकं स्नायुशतं, सप्त शिरासतानि, पञ्च मज्जाशतानि, अस्थीनि च ह वै त्रीणि शतानि षष्टिश्च, अर्धचतस्रो रोमाणि कोट्यः, हृदयं पलान्यष्टौ, द्वादश पलानि जिह्वा, पित्तप्रस्थं, कफस्याढकं, शुक्लं कुडवं, मेदः प्रस्थौ द्वौ, अनियतं मूत्रपुरीषम् आहारपरिमाणात् ॥ पैप्पलादं मोक्षशास्त्रं परिसमाप्तं पैप्पलादं मोक्षशास्त्रं परिसमाप्तमिति ॥ ॐ सह नाववत्विति शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["śarīram iti kasmāt — sākṣād agnayo hy atra śrīyante — jñānāgnir darśanāgniḥ koṣṭhāgnir iti | tatra koṣṭhāgnir nāma aśita-pīta-lehya-coṣyaṃ pacatīti | darśanāgnī rūpādīnāṃ darśanaṃ karoti | jñānāgniḥ śubhāśubhaṃ ca karma vindati | tatra trīṇi sthānāni bhavanti — hṛdaye dakṣiṇāgniḥ, udare gārhapatyaṃ, mukham āhavanīyam | ātmā yajamānaḥ, buddhiṃ patnīṃ nidhāya, mano brahmā, lobhādayaḥ paśavaḥ, dhṛtir dīkṣā, santoṣaś ca, buddhīndriyāṇi yajña-pātrāṇi, karmendriyāṇi havīṃṣi, śiraḥ kapālaṃ, keśā darbhāḥ, mukham antarvediḥ, catuṣkapālaṃ śiraḥ | ṣoḍaśa pārśva-dantoṣṭha-paṭalāni, saptottaraṃ marma-śataṃ, sāśītikaṃ sandhi-śataṃ, sa-navakaṃ snāyu-śataṃ, sapta śirā-śatāni, pañca majjā-śatāni, asthīni ca ha vai trīṇi śatāni ṣaṣṭiś ca, ardha-catasro romāṇi koṭyaḥ, hṛdayaṃ palāny aṣṭau, dvādaśa palāni jihvā, pitta-prasthaṃ, kaphasyāḍhakaṃ, śuklaṃ kuḍavaṃ, medaḥ prasthau dvau, aniyataṃ mūtra-purīṣam āhāra-parimāṇāt | paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstraṃ parisamāptaṃ paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstraṃ parisamāptam iti | oṃ saha nāv avatv iti śāntiḥ ‖"],"words":[["śarīram iti kasmāt","\"why 'śarīra'?\"","\"Why (is the body called) śarīra?\""],["sākṣāt agnayaḥ atra śrīyante","adv. + nom. pl. + pres.","\"because the fires are verily kindled (√śri) here\" (folk-etymology śarīra ← śri)"],["jñāna-agniḥ darśana-agniḥ koṣṭha-agniḥ","nom.","\"the fire of knowledge, the fire of sight, the fire of the belly\""],["koṣṭha-agniḥ … aśita-pīta-lehya-coṣyaṃ pacati","nom. + acc. + pres.","\"the belly-fire cooks what is eaten, drunk, licked, sucked\""],["darśana-agniḥ rūpādīnāṃ darśanaṃ karoti","nom. + gen. + acc.","\"the sight-fire effects the seeing of forms etc.\""],["jñāna-agniḥ śubha-aśubhaṃ karma vindati","nom. + acc.","\"the knowledge-fire knows good and evil deeds\""],["hṛdaye dakṣiṇa-agniḥ","loc. + nom.","\"in the heart, the Southern fire (dakṣiṇāgni)\""],["udare gārhapatyaṃ","loc. + nom.","\"in the belly, the householder's fire (gārhapatya)\""],["mukham āhavanīyam","nom.","\"the mouth (is) the oblation-fire (āhavanīya)\""],["ātmā yajamānaḥ","nom.","\"the Self is the sacrificer\""],["buddhiṃ patnīṃ nidhāya","acc. + abs.","\"having installed intellect as the wife\""],["manaḥ brahmā","nom.","\"mind (is) the Brahman-priest\""],["lobha-ādayaḥ paśavaḥ","nom. pl.","\"greed and the rest are the sacrificial animals\""],["dhṛtiḥ dīkṣā, santoṣaḥ ca","nom.","\"firmness is the consecration, and contentment (the same)\""],["buddhi-indriyāṇi yajña-pātrāṇi","nom. pl.","\"the cognitive senses are the sacrificial vessels\""],["karma-indriyāṇi havīṃṣi","nom. pl.","\"the action-senses are the oblations\""],["śiraḥ kapālaṃ, keśāḥ darbhāḥ","nom.","\"the head is the (sacrificial) bowl, the hairs the darbha-grass\""],["mukham antarvediḥ","nom.","\"the mouth is the inner altar\""],["catuṣkapālaṃ śiraḥ","nom.","\"the head is of four skull-plates\""],["ṣoḍaśa pārśva-danta-oṣṭha-paṭalāni","nom. pl.","\"sixteen are the rows/layers of ribs(?), teeth and lips\" (obscure)"],["saptottaraṃ marma-śataṃ","nom.","\"the vital points number a hundred-and-seven (107)\""],["sāśītikaṃ sandhi-śataṃ","nom.","\"the joints, a hundred-and-eighty (180)\""],["sa-navakaṃ snāyu-śataṃ","nom.","\"the sinews, a-hundred-with-nine (109; var. 900)\""],["sapta śirā-śatāni","nom. pl.","\"the vessels (śirā), seven hundred (700)\""],["pañca majjā-śatāni","nom. pl.","\"the marrow-(units), five hundred (500)\""],["asthīni trīṇi śatāni ṣaṣṭiś ca","nom. pl.","\"the bones, three hundred and sixty (360)\""],["ardha-catasraḥ romāṇi koṭyaḥ","nom.","\"the hairs, three and a half crore (35 million)\""],["hṛdayaṃ palāny aṣṭau","nom.","\"the heart, eight palas (in weight)\""],["dvādaśa palāni jihvā","nom.","\"the tongue, twelve palas\""],["pitta-prasthaṃ","nom.","\"bile, one prastha\""],["kaphasya āḍhakaṃ","gen. + nom.","\"of phlegm, one āḍhaka\""],["śuklaṃ kuḍavaṃ","nom.","\"semen, one kuḍava\""],["medaḥ prasthau dvau","nom.","\"fat, two prasthas\""],["aniyataṃ mūtra-purīṣam āhāra-parimāṇāt","nom. + abl.","\"urine and faeces (are) variable, according to the measure of food\""],["paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstraṃ parisamāptam","nom. + p.p.","\"the liberation-treatise of Pippalāda is concluded\""]],"lit":"\"Why is it (called) <em>śarīra</em>? — because the fires are verily kindled (<em>śrī-yante</em>) here: the fire of knowledge, the fire of sight, the fire of the belly. Of these, the belly-fire (<em>koṣṭhāgni</em>) cooks what is eaten, drunk, licked, and sucked; the sight-fire (<em>darśanāgni</em>) effects the seeing of forms and the rest; the knowledge-fire (<em>jñānāgni</em>) knows deeds good and evil. Therein are three (sacrificial) stations: in the heart the Southern fire, in the belly the householder's fire, the mouth the oblation-fire. The Self is the sacrificer; having installed intellect as the wife; mind is the Brahman-priest; greed and the rest are the victims; firmness is the consecration, and contentment; the cognitive senses are the sacrificial vessels, the action-senses the oblations; the head is the (offering-)bowl, the hairs the darbha-grass, the mouth the inner altar. The head is of four skull-plates; sixteen the rows of ribs, teeth, and lips; the vital points are one hundred and seven; the joints one hundred and eighty; the sinews one hundred and nine (some: nine hundred); the vessels seven hundred; the marrows five hundred; the bones three hundred and sixty; the hairs three and a half crore; the heart weighs eight <em>palas</em>; the tongue twelve <em>palas</em>; bile one <em>prastha</em>; phlegm one <em>āḍhaka</em>; semen one <em>kuḍava</em>; fat two <em>prasthas</em>; urine and faeces are variable, according to the measure of food. The liberation-treatise of Pippalāda is concluded; the liberation-treatise of Pippalāda is concluded. Oṃ — 'May He protect us both' — thus the peace-invocation.\"","para":"The closing section turns from genesis to <strong>constitution and meaning</strong>. First a <em>nirukta</em> (etymology): <em>śarīra</em> (\"body\") is derived from <em>√śri</em> (\"to kindle\"), because three fires \"burn\" in it — the <strong>koṣṭhāgni</strong> (digestive fire that cooks the four kinds of food), the <strong>darśanāgni</strong> (the fire of perception in the eye), and the <strong>jñānāgni</strong> (the fire of conscience that registers good and evil karma). These map onto the <strong>three Vedic sacrificial fires</strong> (<em>dakṣiṇāgni</em> in the heart, <em>gārhapatya</em> in the belly, <em>āhavanīya</em> at the mouth), launching the great <strong>body-as-yajña allegory</strong>: the embodied person <em>is</em> a standing fire-sacrifice — Self as <em>yajamāna</em> (sacrificer), intellect as wife, mind as the officiating <em>brahman</em>-priest, the passions (greed etc.) as the immolated victims, firmness and contentment as the consecratory vows, the senses as the ritual vessels and oblations, the very skull and hair as bowl and sacred grass. The Upaniṣad then closes with its astonishing <strong>anatomical census</strong> — a numbered inventory of the body's components (107 <em>marmas</em>, 180 joints, 700 vessels, 360 bones, 3.5 crore hairs) and the weights of the inner substances (heart 8 <em>palas</em>, tongue 12, bile a <em>prastha</em>, etc.) — a catalogue that is simultaneously a feat of proto-anatomy and, in the light of the opening maṅgala, the final and most thorough exercise in <em>śarīrāpahnava</em>: to count the body exhaustively is to see through it. The double colophon seals the text as the <em>mokṣa-śāstra</em> of Pippalāda.","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","makes the <em>yajña</em>-allegory the doctrinal summit: the body rightly understood is an unceasing sacrifice in which the passions are offered up and the Self presides — the same <em>adhyātma-yajña</em> taught in Bhagavad-Gītā 4.24–30 and the <em>prāṇāgnihotra</em> tradition. The three <em>agnis</em> are the digestive, perceptual, and moral \"fires\" of the living organism"],["The Āyurvedic embryology","matches the census against Suśruta <em>Śārīrasthāna</em> 5: Suśruta agrees exactly on the <strong>107 <em>marmas<strong><em> (vital points) — the foundation of Āyurvedic surgery — but gives </strong>300 bones<strong> (against Garbha's 360, a count closer to the older Atharvavedic tradition), </strong>210 joints"],[", and","700 </em>śirā</em></strong> (matching Garbha); the divergences place Garbha alongside, but not identical to, the medical mainstream"],["Paul Deussen","notes the <em>śarīra ← śri</em> etymology as a typical Upaniṣadic <em>nirvacana</em> and the census as the text's \"anatomical appendix.\""],["K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar","renders the numbers and the <em>yajña</em>-correspondences in full"],["Sivananda / Krishnananda","reads the body-sacrifice as the practical upshot of the whole Upaniṣad: having anatomized and renounced the body, one re-consecrates it as the altar on which the ego is offered"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Synthesis","title":"Details across the whole Upaniṣad","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>A medical text inside a mokṣa-text.</strong> Garbha is unique in the canon for embedding a full <strong>embryological and anatomical treatise</strong> within a soteriology. The frame (maṅgala + §4 lament + §5 <em>yajña</em>) is Vedāntic-renunciate; the core (§1–§3, §5 census) is essentially <strong>Āyurvedic science</strong>. The two are bound by the maṅgala's thesis — <em>śarīrāpahnava</em>, the discrediting of body-identity: one studies the body in clinical detail precisely in order to dis-identify from it.","- <strong>The numerical skeleton.</strong> The whole work hangs on the opening sūtra's number-series — <strong>5</strong> elements, <strong>5</strong> sense-spheres, <strong>6</strong> tastes/bases, <strong>6</strong> qualities, <strong>7</strong> tissues, <strong>3</strong> wastes, <strong>2</strong> parental sources, <strong>4</strong> foods — each later expounded. This mnemonic architecture is characteristic of the late, systematizing Upaniṣads.","- <strong>Embedded Sāṃkhya-Yoga.</strong> §3's \"eight <em>prakṛtis</em> and sixteen <em>vikāras</em>\" (the 24 <em>tattvas</em>) and §4's first refuge-vow (\"I will take refuge in Sāṃkhya and Yoga\") show classical Sāṃkhya-Yoga fully formed; the embryo is even said to meditate on <strong>Oṃ</strong> <em>in utero</em>.","- <strong>Ecumenical soteriology.</strong> The fourfold vow of §4 — Sāṃkhya-Yoga, Maheśvara (Śiva), Nārāyaṇa (Viṣṇu), and nirguṇa Brahman — deliberately spans jñāna, Śaiva bhakti, Vaiṣṇava bhakti, and Advaita. The Maheśvara and Nārāyaṇa vows are likely later sectarian insertions into an older Sāṃkhya-Yoga-plus-Brahman pair (so De).","- <strong>The amnesia of birth.</strong> The doctrinal pivot is the <strong>vaiṣṇava vāyu</strong>: the soul's saving recollection (§3) is erased the instant it is born (§4). Bondage, for Garbha, is constituted by <em>forgetting</em>; liberation would be the <em>re-collection</em> of what the birth-wind wiped away — making memory itself the spiritual battleground.","- <strong>The Atharvan signature.</strong> The closing <em>paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstram</em> ties the text to the Paippalāda Atharvaveda and to the <em>prāṇāgnihotra</em>/body-sacrifice current of the Atharvan and later Upaniṣads."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Visual representation","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>GARBHA UPANIṢAD — THE ARCHITECTURE OF THE BODY &amp; ITS RELEASE\n\n MAṄGALA: &quot;śarīrāpahnavāt siddham&quot; — know the Self by SUBLATING the body\n        │\n ┌──────┴───────────────── THE SIXFOLD BODY (opening sūtra) ─────────────────┐\n │  5 elements · 5 sense-spheres · 6 tastes · 6 qualities ·                    │\n │  7 dhātus · 3 malas · 2 parents · 4 foods                                   │\n └──────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n        │\n §1  ELEMENTS  → earth/water/fire/air/ether  (hard/liquid/hot/moving/hollow)\n     SENSES    → ear·skin·eye·tongue·nose·genitals·anus + buddhi/manas/vāc\n     6 TASTES (ṣaḍ-rasa) · 7 NOTES (Sa Ri Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni)\n        │\n §2  7 COLOURS → white·red·black·smoky·yellow·tawny·pale\n     DHĀTU-CHAIN:  food→ rasa→ blood→ flesh→ fat→ sinew→ bone→ marrow→ ŚUKRA\n                   śukra + śoṇita ⟹ EMBRYO seated in the HEART\n        │\n §3  EMBRYOGENESIS (by lunar month)\n     day1 kalila → day7 budbuda → ½mo piṇḍa → 1mo hardens →\n     2 head · 3 feet · 4 belly/hips · 5 spine · 6 face+senses ·\n     7 JĪVA joins · 8 complete\n     ┌ sex:  father-seed↑ = male · mother-seed↑ = female · equal = napuṃsaka ┐\n     └ 24 tattvas in the body · meditates OṂ · 9th mo → REMEMBERS PAST LIVES ┘\n        │\n §4  ★ THE WOMB-LAMENT ★  &quot;sunk in an ocean of sorrow, I see no remedy&quot;\n     fourfold VOW if released:  SĀṂKHYA-YOGA → MAHEŚVARA → NĀRĀYAṆA → BRAHMAN\n        │\n     BIRTH:  squeezed &quot;as by a press&quot; → touched by the VAIṢṆAVA VĀYU\n        │\n     ▶▶ FORGETS birth, death, and all karma ◀◀   (the cycle reseals)\n        │\n §5  WHY &quot;ŚARĪRA&quot;?  ← √śri, &quot;to kindle&quot;:  3 fires\n     koṣṭhāgni (digest) · darśanāgni (sight) · jñānāgni (conscience)\n     ┌──────── THE BODY AS YAJÑA ────────┐\n     │ Self = sacrificer · buddhi = wife  │     3 fires ≙ dakṣiṇa (heart) /\n     │ manas = brahman-priest             │              gārhapatya (belly) /\n     │ greed&amp;c. = victims · senses = vessels │            āhavanīya (mouth)\n     └────────────────────────────────────┘\n     CENSUS: 107 marmas · 180 joints · 700 vessels · 500 marrows ·\n             360 bones · 3.5 crore hairs · heart 8 pala · tongue 12 pala …\n        │\n COLOPHON: &quot;paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstraṃ parisamāptam&quot; (×2)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical steps and observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Read the anatomy as <em>vairāgya</em>, not biology (maṅgala, §5).</strong> The Upaniṣad's own instruction is that the embryology and the bone-count serve <em>śarīrāpahnava</em> — the loosening of body-identity. Used rightly, the detailed catalogue is a meditation: \"this counted, weighed, perishable aggregate is <em>had</em>, not <em>am</em>.\"","2. <strong>The womb-recollection contemplation (§3–§4).</strong> Sit with the text's central scenario: a being that, for one lucid moment, sees the whole sorrowful round and vows to seek release — then forgets at birth. Ask: <em>what have I forgotten?</em> The spiritual life, on Garbha's logic, is the deliberate re-collection of the clarity the \"birth-wind\" erased.","3. <strong>Honour the four roads (§4).</strong> The fourfold vow legitimizes four paths — discriminative Sāṃkhya-Yoga, Śaiva devotion, Vaiṣṇava devotion, and nirguṇa Brahman-meditation. Garbha's counsel is non-sectarian: any of these \"destroys demerit and bestows liberation.\" Choose the road that fits your temperament without disparaging the others.","4. <strong>Tend the three fires (§5).</strong> Watch the <em>koṣṭhāgni</em> (digestion — eat to keep it clean and even, the Āyurvedic <em>agni</em>), the <em>darśanāgni</em> (perception — guard what you take in through the senses), and especially the <em>jñānāgni</em> (conscience — the inner fire that \"knows good and evil karma\"). Garbha makes conscience a literal fire to be kept burning, not dulled.","5. <strong>Live as a standing sacrifice (§5).</strong> Enact the <em>yajña</em>-allegory: let the Self preside as <em>yajamāna</em>, offer the passions (<em>lobhādayaḥ paśavaḥ</em>) as the victims, and keep <em>dhṛti</em> (firmness) and <em>santoṣa</em> (contentment) as your standing vows. Ordinary bodily life, so framed, becomes <em>adhyātma-yajña</em> — the continuous oblation of the ego.","6. <strong>Eat and gestate mindfully (§1–§3).</strong> For the embodied and especially the expectant: the text's ancient counsel — that the parents' \"disturbed mind\" (<em>vyākulita-manas</em>) at conception shapes the child, and that the foetus is nourished and \"ensouled\" through the mother — is a call to equanimity, purity, and care around conception, pregnancy, and the first nourishment."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Bibliography","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>Primary text &amp; translations</strong>","- <em>Garbhopaniṣat</em>, ITRANS e-text `garbha.itx` (transcr. Sunder Hattangadi), sanskritdocuments.org — base text for this edition.","- <strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong>, <em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em> (Madras, 1914) — English translation of Garbha with notes (the standard reference).","- <strong>Paul Deussen</strong>, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (Leipzig, 1897); Eng. tr. Bedekar &amp; Palsule, <em>Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda</em> (Delhi, 1980) — <em>Garbha-Upaniṣad</em> with introduction and notes.","- <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong>, <em>Dīpikā</em> on the 108 Upaniṣads, ed. Adyar Library — the sole classical Sanskrit commentary.","<strong>Medical (Āyurveda) parallels</strong>","- <em>Caraka-Saṃhitā</em>, <em>Śārīrasthāna</em> (esp. ch. 3–4: <em>khuḍḍikā/mahatī garbhāvakrānti</em> — conception and month-by-month development).","- <em>Suśruta-Saṃhitā</em>, <em>Śārīrasthāna</em> (esp. ch. 3 <em>garbhāvakrānti</em>; ch. 5 <em>śarīra-saṃkhyā-vyākaraṇa</em> — the count of bones, joints, <em>marmas</em>, <em>śirā</em>).","- <strong>Rahul Peter Das</strong>, <em>The Origin of the Life of a Human Being: Conception and the Female according to Ancient Indian Medical and Sexological Literature</em> (Delhi, 2003).","- <strong>Dominik Wujastyk</strong>, <em>The Roots of Āyurveda</em> (Penguin, 2003) — on the medical-text tradition.","<strong>Modern Vedānta</strong>","- <strong>Swami Sivananda / Swami Krishnananda</strong> (Divine Life Society), writings on the minor Upaniṣads — thematic Vedāntic reading."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references within the Upaniṣadic & Sanskrit corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Garbha locus</td><td>Parallel / source</td><td>Note</td></tr><tr><td>Opening sūtra (5 elements, 7 dhātus, 3 malas)</td><td>Caraka <em>Sūtra</em>/<em>Śārīra</em>; Suśruta <em>Śārīra</em></td><td>the Āyurvedic definition of <em>śarīra</em></td></tr><tr><td>§1 element-definitions (hard/liquid/hot/moving/hollow)</td><td>Taittirīya 2.1 (elements from Self); Sāṃkhya-Kārikā 22</td><td>functional physics of the <em>mahābhūtas</em></td></tr><tr><td>§1 six tastes (<em>ṣaḍ-rasa</em>)</td><td>Caraka <em>Sūtra</em> 1; Chāndogya 6.4 (colours of food)</td><td>nutrition as the basis of body</td></tr><tr><td>§2 dhātu-chain (rasa→…→śukra)</td><td>Suśruta <em>Śārīra</em> 4; Caraka <em>Cikitsā</em> 15</td><td><em>dhātu-pariṇāma</em> doctrine</td></tr><tr><td>§2 embryo seated in the heart</td><td>Maitri 2.6, 6.30; Kaṭha 2.3.17 (<em>hṛd-guhā</em>)</td><td>the heart-cave as seat of the embodied self</td></tr><tr><td>§3 <em>kalila → budbuda → piṇḍa</em></td><td>Suśruta <em>Śārīra</em> 3; Caraka <em>Śārīra</em> 4; Garbhāvakrānti-sūtra (Buddhist)</td><td>shared embryogenic stages</td></tr><tr><td>§3 sex by seed-dominance</td><td>Suśruta <em>Śārīra</em> 2; Manu 3.49; BĀU 6.4.21</td><td>the <em>retas</em>-dominance theory</td></tr><tr><td>§3 <em>aṣṭau prakṛtayaḥ ṣoḍaśa vikārāḥ</em></td><td>Sāṃkhya-Kārikā 3, 22; BhG 7.4</td><td>the 24 <em>tattvas</em></td></tr><tr><td>§3 embryo meditates on <strong>Oṃ</strong></td><td>Māṇḍūkya 1; Praśna 5; Maitri 6.3–6.5</td><td>praṇava as the syllable of the Self</td></tr><tr><td>§3 ninth-month recollection of past lives</td><td>Yoga-Sūtra 2.39 (<em>jāti-smara</em>); Mahābhārata Śānti-parva</td><td><em>pūrva-jāti-smaraṇa</em></td></tr><tr><td>§4 womb-lament (<em>duḥkhodadhau magnaḥ</em>)</td><td>Manu 6.61–62; Mahābhārata 12; Buddhist/Jain garbha-duḥkha topoi</td><td>the misery-of-the-womb genre</td></tr><tr><td>§4 fourfold vow (Sāṃkhya-Yoga / Śiva / Viṣṇu / Brahman)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara (theistic synthesis); BhG 7–12</td><td>ecumenical <em>mokṣa-mārgas</em></td></tr><tr><td>§4 <em>vaiṣṇava vāyu</em> / birth-amnesia</td><td>Maitri 2.6 (entry of breath); Garbhāvakrānti (Buddhist)</td><td>the forgetting at birth</td></tr><tr><td>§5 <em>śarīra ← √śri</em> (fires kindled)</td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.1 (folk-etymology style); Aitareya 1</td><td>Upaniṣadic <em>nirvacana</em></td></tr><tr><td>§5 body-as-<em>yajña</em></td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 4.24–30; Prāṇāgnihotra-Up.; Chāndogya 5.18–24 (Vaiśvānara)</td><td>the inner/self-sacrifice (<em>adhyātma-yajña</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>§5 three fires (dakṣiṇa/gārhapatya/āhavanīya)</td><td>Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa; Chāndogya 5.19–24</td><td>the three śrauta fires internalized</td></tr><tr><td>§5 anatomical census (107 marmas, 360 bones …)</td><td>Suśruta <em>Śārīra</em> 5; Atharvaveda 10.2 (<em>kuṭastha</em> body-hymn)</td><td>the <em>śarīra-saṃkhyā</em> (body-enumeration) tradition</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Thus the complete Garbha Upaniṣad — the maṅgala and śānti; the sixfold definition of the body; the five elements, senses, six tastes and seven notes (§1); the seven colours, the dhātu-chain and the seating of the embryo in the heart (§2); the month-by-month embryogenesis, the determination of sex, the in-utero meditation on Oṃ and the ninth-month recollection of past lives (§3); the lament in the womb, the fourfold vow of refuge, and the amnesia of the Vaiṣṇava wind at birth (§4); and the etymology of \"śarīra,\" the three fires, the body-as-sacrifice, and the anatomical census (§5) — the</em> paippalādaṃ mokṣa-śāstram, <em>concluded. Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
